<script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(articleSchema)} />
<header class="re-art-hero"><div class="container"><a class="re-art-back" href="/insights/">INSIGHTS / REAL ESTATE</a><h1>Google Ads for Realtors: Campaigns, Keywords and Lead Quality</h1><p>A practical guide to Google Ads for realtors covering buyer and seller intent, commercial property, relevant landing pages and qualified inquiries.</p></div></header><article class="re-art-body"><aside><strong>THE SHORT ANSWER</strong><p>Google Ads for realtors works best when campaigns separate buyer, seller, listing and property-type intent, then connect each inquiry to a relevant agent and measurable next step.</p></aside><div class="re-art-cta"><strong>LEADSFLO / REAL ESTATE</strong><p>Explore connected advertising, landing pages and CRM follow-up.</p><a href="/real-estate-lead-generation/">See Real Estate Services ↗</a></div><section><h2>Start with the client, not just the keyword</h2><p>An active homebuyer, homeowner considering a sale, office tenant and commercial investor are searching for different things. The campaigns should reflect their motivations and the brokerage&#x27;s actual market coverage.</p><p>Define the service area, available property categories and conversion objective before building ad groups. A campaign promoting listing consultations should not send inquiries to a generic homes-for-sale page.</p></section>
<section><h2>Research real demand and separate search intent</h2><p>Buyer searches may concern a location or property category. Seller searches may involve local brokerage services and selling consultations. Commercial search themes can involve office leasing, retail premises, warehouses and property investments.</p><p>Use real Keyword Planner and search term data for the client&#x27;s market. Exclude irrelevant research and property searches that do not align with the campaign objective. Avoid treating agency-service keywords as patient or property-consumer terms.</p></section>
<section><h2>Make the landing page relevant</h2><p>A good landing page repeats the specific service or offer from the ad, explains the next step and provides enough information for someone to decide whether to inquire.</p><p>For property-specific campaigns use approved listing assets and verify availability. For seller consultations explain what the agent can discuss without guaranteeing prices or selling times.</p></section>
<section><h2>Review advertising and housing compliance</h2><p>US housing ads are subject to platform restrictions and applicable fair housing obligations. Google restricts certain demographic and ZIP-code targeting for relevant housing advertising; check the current policy when setting up campaigns.</p><p>Classify commercial and adjacent services according to the actual offer, rather than assuming that every real estate campaign receives the same treatment. Avoid discriminatory claims and unsupported audience exclusions.</p></section>
<section><h2>Measure leads through the CRM</h2><p>Record form submissions, calls and booking requests as different events. Route each inquiry to an accountable agent, then record contact attempts, useful conversations and appointments confirmed by the team.</p><p>Compare qualified conversations by campaign, property type and geography. A cheap form fill is not proof of a sales opportunity, and a booking-link click is not a completed consultation.</p></section>
<section><h2>Improve one conversion stage at a time</h2><p>Review actual search terms, landing-page relevance, lead validity and response speed. Poor contact rates may reflect the follow-up process rather than ad targeting alone.</p><p>Expand only when tracking and agent capacity are reliable. Avoid publishing invented cost-per-lead benchmarks or implying that campaign performance guarantees listings or closed transactions.</p></section><div class="re-art-related"><h2>Related resources</h2><a href="/insights/real-estate-lead-generation/">Real Estate Lead Generation ↗</a><a href="/insights/facebook-ads-for-realtors/">Facebook Ads for Realtors ↗</a><a href="/insights/real-estate-seller-leads/">Real Estate Seller Leads ↗</a><a href="/real-estate-lead-generation/">Real Estate Marketing Services ↗</a></div>
<!-- AI_CONTENT_REAL_ESTATE_V3 -->
<div class="re-ai-editorial">
<section><h2>Recover inquiries from calls and landing pages</h2><p>Search campaigns can generate direct phone calls as well as forms. A clearly identified AI voice receptionist can assist with permitted call handling, collect the reason for the inquiry and request a callback or transfer when a human agent is unavailable.</p><p>For online forms, a CRM workflow can retain campaign attribution and route the inquiry to the appropriate team. The assistant should use the advertised service and property context rather than send an unrelated generic response.</p></section>
<section><h2>Measure the complete advertising-to-appointment journey</h2><p>Separate ad clicks, phone calls, form submissions, reached contacts and confirmed meetings in reporting. An AI conversation may identify a useful inquiry, but a qualified opportunity still requires appropriate business verification.</p><p>Use missed-call recovery, permission-aware messaging and assigned-agent tasks to reduce operational gaps. Review current housing-ad policies and avoid claiming that automation guarantees leads, appointments or transactions.</p></section>
